<reference_puzzle index="1" source="eval" puzzle_id="8698868d" lines="9-23">
<description>
Match sprites to frames by connected-hole count, then nest each sprite inside its frame.

Input structure:
- bg = azure (8). Large hollow squares (frames) arranged in a grid; smaller filled squares (sprites) placed elsewhere.
- Each frame and sprite has a distinct color. Sprites may contain holes (non-sprite-color pixels).

Rule:
- Frames form a regular grid; some may be bg-colored (invisible).
- Count connected hole regions inside each frame's interior and each sprite.
- Pair each frame with the sprite sharing the same hole count.
- Place the matched sprite inside the frame border; recolor sprite holes to the frame's color.

Key insight: The matching key is the number of connected hole components, not hole area or color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="db615bd4" lines="111-130">
<description>
Thicken the sparse frame and stamp the external sparse sprites into it in positional order.

Input structure
The grid has a dominant background color, a regular lattice color, one large
sparse rectangular frame, and one or more external monochrome sprites whose
pixels also sit on a two-step lattice.

Rule
Use the largest sparse rectangular perimeter as the frame. Clear that frame
region and the external sprite pixels to background, draw a solid one-pixel
border in the frame color, solidify each sparse sprite into its contiguous
two-step mask, and place the sprites inside the frame with equal gaps.
Wide frames receive sprites left-to-right by source position; tall frames
receive them top-to-bottom by source position.

Key insight
The frame supplies ordered slots: the sprite-to-slot assignment is by visible
source order, not by numeric color or by matching individual cells.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="eval" puzzle_id="446ef5d2" lines="8-20">
<description>
Assemble scattered rectangular pieces into a rectangle at an L-marker corner.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Non-bg rectangular pieces scattered on the grid.
- 3-pixel L-marker (2x2 minus one cell) tags the corner; the gap is the corner cell.

Rule:
- rect_col = most common color across all piece pixels; all outer rectangle edges must be rect_col.
- Translate pieces (no rotation) to tile the rectangle; touching edges must match, with rect_col as wildcard.

Key insight: rect_col acts as wildcard during edge matching — only non-rect_col pixels at shared boundaries must agree.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="1efba499" lines="70-85">
<description>
Swap paired marker pixels across the largest foreground-color barrier, placing them just outside the barrier segment.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- One foreground color is the unique most frequent non-background color; its pixels form the stationary barrier.
- Other foreground pixels are single markers, sometimes paired on opposite sides of one contiguous barrier segment in the same row or column.

Rule:
- Scan each row and column that crosses exactly one contiguous barrier segment.
- If the line has exactly one marker on each side of that segment, erase both source markers and place each marker in the cell immediately beyond the opposite side of the segment.
- Lines with markers on only one side are unchanged; the barrier remains unchanged.

Key insight:
- The marker pairing is local to a row or column, and the destination is defined by the nearest cells outside the intervening barrier segment, not by the original distance from the barrier.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="8886d717" lines="43-63">
<description>
Repair markers embedded in the main object and copy the remaining markers one pixel toward the solid edge.

Input structure:
- A mostly background grid contains one solid non-background edge line.
- Apart from that edge and the background, there is one large main object
  color and a smaller marker color.
- Each marker is either embedded in the main object, touching it on at
  least three 4-neighbor sides, or isolated on the background.

Rule:
- Embedded markers are recolored to the main object color.
- Isolated background markers remain in place and also paint the adjacent
  background pixel in the direction of the solid edge line.
- All other pixels are unchanged.

Key insight:
The full edge line is a direction cue: it tells which way the background
markers extend, while markers surrounded by the main object are simply
defects to be filled.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="ad173014" lines="97-111">
<description>
Recolor each framed interior mask with the color from the next red frame clockwise around the blue connector cycle.

Input structure
- The grid contains hollow red square frames connected pairwise by blue line components.
- The blue components connect the frames into one cycle, with every frame touching exactly two blue components.
- Each frame interior contains background plus exactly one non-background color mask.

Rule
- Sort the red frames clockwise by their centers around the cycle.
- Keep each frame's interior mask shape in place, but recolor it with the interior color from the next clockwise frame.
- Leave the red frames, blue connectors, background, and interior background holes unchanged.

Key insight
- The colored masks do not move; the blue cycle gives the order in which their colors rotate.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="bae5c565" lines="10-28">
<description>
Erase the top palette row and redraw it below as a widening wedge centered on its missing cell.

Input structure
- The top row is a palette row with exactly one background gap.
- Below it, the only foreground is one vertical marker bar of a single color.
- The marker bar is either in the gap column or immediately beside it.

Rule
- Clear the grid to background.
- Starting at the top of the marker bar, each next row copies one more
  column of the top-row palette on both sides of the gap.
- Fill the gap column with the marker color, and keep the vertical marker bar.

Key insight
- The lone background cell in the top row is the center of the expanding
  copy; the lower vertical bar provides the center color and the row where
  the expansion begins.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="eval" puzzle_id="da515329" lines="11-24">
<description>
Grow four clockwise spiral wall paths from the tips of a teal cross.

Input structure:
- bg = 0 (black). A single teal (8) cross centered in the grid.

Rule:
- Each arm tip launches a "turtle" heading 90° right of its outward direction.
- Movement priority: turn right > go straight > turn left (left only if not on border).
- Normal step = 2 cells; step right after a turn = 1 cell.
- Each new cell must have a 1-cell orthogonal gap from all existing walls except the predecessor.

Key insight: The right-turn preference plus alternating 2/1 step lengths creates self-avoiding spirals that tile the grid with uniform spacing.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="83eb0a57" lines="72-86">
<description>
Crop the largest object and overlay each smaller sprite where its shared marker pattern matches.

Input structure
- The background is the most common color, and the foreground has one largest destination object plus smaller source sprites.
- Each source sprite shares exactly one marker color with the destination object.

Rule
- Output the tight bbox crop of the largest object.
- Translate each source sprite so every connected marker component of the shared color exactly matches destination marker components.
- Paste source sprites in decreasing area order, so smaller sprites can sit on top of larger ones when they overlap.

Key insight
- The shared-color marker components are alignment keys; matching whole marker components distinguishes singleton slots from larger same-color marker blocks.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="eval" puzzle_id="dbff022c" lines="275-288">
<description>
Fill hollow sprites' interiors using a color-palette legend on the grid edge.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Several hollow sprites with distinct border colors.
- A small 2×N or N×2 palette block sits on one grid edge; one row/column lists border colors, the other lists corresponding fill colors.

Rule:
- Locate the palette legend on a grid edge and read it as a border→fill color mapping.
- Find enclosed bg regions (holes) inside sprites that don't touch the grid boundary.
- For each hole, identify the most common adjacent border color, look it up in the palette, and fill the hole with the mapped fill color.

Key insight: The palette's edge position and orientation determine reading direction; border colors in the palette uniquely key into fill colors.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="60d73be6" lines="22-39">
<description>
Complete the sparse colored marker pattern by reflecting it across a full-row/full-column cross.

Input structure
The grid has a dominant background and one non-background cross color that
fills exactly one complete row and one complete column. All other foreground
pixels are sparse markers away from that cross.

Rule
Keep the input unchanged, and for each marker pixel copy its color to the
positions obtained by reflecting it across the cross row, across the cross
column, and across both axes. Reflected positions outside the grid are
clipped.

Key insight
The full cross is not a copied object; it is the pair of mirror axes for
completing the quadrant pattern.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="train" puzzle_id="37ce87bb" lines="33-47">
<description>
Compute a grey result bar by adding teal bar heights and subtracting red bar heights.

Input structure
- The grid has a dominant background and several bottom-aligned one-cell-wide vertical bars.
- Teal bars are positive operands; red bars are negative operands.
- The penultimate column is an empty result slot.

Rule
- Keep every input bar unchanged.
- Compute result height = total teal bar height - total red bar height.
- Paint that many bottom cells of the penultimate column grey.

Key insight
- The bars encode arithmetic by height, with color providing the sign.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="train" puzzle_id="9f5f939b" lines="42-57">
<description>
Mark the missing center of each plus-shaped arrangement of four two-pixel bars with yellow.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- The single foreground color forms isolated 4-connected objects, each a horizontal or vertical two-pixel bar.
- Some bars form an implied plus sign: two horizontal bars and two vertical bars around an empty center, with a one-pixel gap between the center and each bar.

Rule:
- Keep the input unchanged except at implied plus-sign centers.
- For every background pixel with foreground bars at offsets 2-3 cells left, right, above, and below, recolor that center yellow.
- The one-pixel gaps immediately next to the center in the four cardinal directions must remain background.

Key insight:
- The yellow pixels are not extending a line; they identify the shared missing center where four separated two-pixel bars align as a plus sign.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

